---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import j from '../../../data/janghan.json';

const E = '/janghan';           // the research interface (static, generated from the frozen corpus)
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Janghan (長恨, 1927): A Digital Scholarly Edition of a Gisaeng-Edited Magazine',
  description: 'Reconstructed corpus, structural encoding and coded spatial evidence for the two 1927 issues of the Korean magazine 長恨, built from four independent witnesses.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal Khan' },
  temporalCoverage: '1927', inLanguage: 'ko',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
});
---
<Project title="長恨 (1927) — A Gisaeng-Edited Magazine"
  description="A digital scholarly edition of 長恨 (Janghan, 1927), the only magazine edited and largely written by Korean gisaeng, reconstructed from four independent witnesses.">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={jsonld}></script>
  <style>
    .meta-line{font:var(--t-caption)/1.6 var(--sans);color:var(--muted);
      border-top:1px solid var(--rule);border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);
      padding:.55rem 0;margin:var(--s-5) 0 var(--s-6)}
    .covers{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1.6rem;margin:var(--s-6) 0}
    .cover{margin:0}
    .cover img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block;border:1px solid var(--rule);background:var(--card)}
    .cover figcaption{font:var(--t-caption)/1.55 var(--sans);color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem}
    section.act{border-top:1px solid var(--rule);margin-top:var(--s-7);padding-top:.2rem}
    .act h2 .act-no{font:600 var(--t-label)/1 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.13em;color:var(--accent);
      display:block;margin-bottom:.45rem;text-transform:uppercase}
    .explore{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(215px,1fr));gap:1rem;margin:var(--s-5) 0}
    .ex-card{display:block;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:1rem 1.1rem;
      background:var(--card);text-decoration:none;color:inherit}
    .ex-card:hover{border-color:var(--accent)}
    .ex-card h3{margin:0 0 .3rem;font-size:1rem}
    .ex-card p{margin:0;font:var(--t-caption)/1.55 var(--sans);color:var(--muted)}
    .ontology{border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:var(--radius);background:var(--card);padding:var(--s-5) 1.4rem;margin:var(--s-5) 0}
    .ont-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:170px 1fr;gap:.5rem 1.2rem;padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
    .ont-row:last-child{border-bottom:0}
    .ont-row dt{font:600 .9rem var(--sans)}
    .ont-row dd{margin:0;font-size:.96rem;color:var(--muted)}
    .tl{margin:var(--s-5) 0;padding-left:1.5rem;border-left:2px solid var(--rule);list-style:none}
    .tl li{position:relative;padding:0 0 1.15rem}
    .tl li::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:-1.93rem;top:.42rem;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%;
      background:var(--paper);border:2px solid var(--accent)}
    .tl b{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem}
    .tl p{margin:.2rem 0 0;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted)}
    .method{border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:var(--s-5) 1.4rem;
      font-size:.95rem;background:var(--card)}
    .method p{margin-bottom:.8rem}.method p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
    .caveat{border-left:3px solid var(--negative);padding:.2rem 0 .2rem 1.15rem;margin:var(--s-5) 0;font-size:.95rem}
    .caveat .cap-kicker{font:600 var(--t-label)/1.4 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;
      color:var(--negative);margin:0 0 .35rem}
    .caveat p{margin:0 0 .5rem}.caveat p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
    @media (max-width:640px){.covers{grid-template-columns:1fr}.ont-row{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.1rem}}
  </style>

  <div class="wrap" style="padding-top:2.8rem">
    <p class="eyebrow">Digital humanities · Colonial Korean print culture</p>
    <h1>長恨 (1927): the magazine the gisaeng made</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      For two issues in 1927, women registered as gisaeng edited and largely wrote their own magazine.
      This is a digital scholarly edition of both issues — reconstructed from four independent witnesses
      that do not agree on what the magazine contained.
    </p>
    <p class="meta-line">
      {j.issue1.units + j.issue2.units} editorial units · {j.issue1.items + j.issue2.items} textual items ·
      {j.contributors} bylines · {j.witnesses} witnesses reconciled · Issue 1 corpus frozen ·
      every claim opens to its source
    </p>

    <div class="covers">
      <figure class="cover">
        <img src={`${E}/assets/jh01-cover.png`} width="900" height="1470"
             alt="Cover of 長恨 issue 1, January 1927: a woman seated inside a birdcage, with the title in two circular seals above." />
        <figcaption><b>창간호</b> · 10 January 1927 — <span lang="ko">동무여 생각하라 조롱 속에 이 몸을</span><br />
          “Comrades, think of me here inside the cage.” From the 1927 original.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cover">
        <img src={`${E}/assets/jh02-cover.png`} width="411" height="606"
             alt="Cover of 長恨 issue 2, February 1927: two women, one in hanbok and one in Western dress, holding hands beneath a rising sun." />
        <figcaption><b>2월호</b> · February 1927 — a woman in hanbok and a woman in Western dress,
          hands joined beneath a rising sun. Reproduced in the 2009 edition.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section class="act">
      <h2><span class="act-no">The problem</span>No two sources agree on what is in it</h2>
      <p>
        長恨 was published by 長恨社 in Kyŏngsŏng, with a gisaeng named as publisher and the proletarian
        writer 최학송 editing in fact. It ran for two issues. Almost everything else about its contents is
        contested: the published bibliography records {j.literature.issue1} and {j.literature.issue2} articles,
        the Yonsei catalogue lists {j.yonsei.issue1} and {j.yonsei.issue2}, the 1927 table of contents lists a
        curated selection, and a 2009 reprint numbers its own sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those figures are not rival claims about the same thing. They count different units — chiefly poems
        printed inside other people’s articles, and prompts that carry several signed answers. This edition
        holds the disagreement open instead of resolving it silently, and shows how each witness divides the
        magazine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="act">
      <h2><span class="act-no">Explore</span>The magazine</h2>
      <p>
        The research interface below is generated directly from the frozen corpus, so it cannot drift from
        the data. Every item, contributor and place opens to the evidence behind it.
      </p>
      <div class="explore">
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/`}><h3>Enter the edition →</h3>
          <p>Covers, both issues, and the full apparatus.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/contents.html`}><h3>Contents, three ways</h3>
          <p>Switch between the 1927 list, the catalogue, and this reconstruction.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/issue/jh01.html`}><h3>{j.issue1.label} · Issue 1</h3>
          <p>{j.issue1.items} textual items in {j.issue1.units} editorial units, {j.issue1.date}.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/issue/jh02.html`}><h3>{j.issue2.label} · Issue 2</h3>
          <p>{j.issue2.items} textual items in {j.issue2.units} editorial units, {j.issue2.date}.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/architecture.html`}><h3>Editorial architecture</h3>
          <p>Symposia, a round-table, serials, recurring columns, nested poems.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/contributors.html`}><h3>Contributors</h3>
          <p>{j.contributors} bylines; {j.cross_issue_contributors} recur across both issues.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/places.html`}><h3>Places</h3>
          <p>{j.place_mentions} coded mentions across {j.places} places — {j.place_mentions_unmappable} of them unmappable.</p></a>
        <a class="ex-card" href={`${E}/witnesses.html`}><h3>Witnesses</h3>
          <p>What each of the four sources attests, item by item.</p></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="act">
      <h2><span class="act-no">Structure</span>Three levels, because counting depends on the level</h2>
      <p>
        The edition records the magazine at three levels. Keeping them apart is what makes the witnesses
        comparable at all.
      </p>
      <dl class="ontology">
        <div class="ont-row"><dt>Bibliographic entry</dt>
          <dd>A record in an external catalogue. A witness to the contents — never this edition’s own unit.</dd></div>
        <div class="ont-row"><dt>Editorial unit</dt>
          <dd>An item as the magazine presents it: one article, one column, or one prompt with its answers.
            Counts are reported at this level.</dd></div>
        <div class="ont-row"><dt>Textual item</dt>
          <dd>A single continuous text with its own title or byline. Each answer to a prompt, and each poem
            printed inside another article, is one. {j.nested_items} are nested inside a parent unit.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="act">
      <h2><span class="act-no">Reconstruction</span>How the contents were rebuilt</h2>
      <ul class="tl">
        <li><b>An inherited baseline</b>
          <p>58 records across both issues, with approximate page ranges and no external check.</p></li>
        <li><b>Reading every candidate at source</b>
          <p>Three earlier passes had disagreed on how many articles were recoverable. Re-reading each at the
            page settled it — and turned up a two-part serialised novel that no inventory had recorded.</p></li>
        <li><b>Adding an independent witness</b>
          <p>The Yonsei catalogue was reconciled against the text. One inherited record proved to be a phantom;
            another was re-identified as a poem.</p></li>
        <li><b>Recovering the page itself</b>
          <p>The 1927 scan showed that Issue 1 is set in two registers — a main upper block and a running
            bottom strip of short items, marked in the magazine’s own table of contents.</p></li>
        <li><b>Freezing Issue 1, then reconciling Issue 2</b>
          <p>Issue 1 is frozen at {j.issue1.units} editorial units. Issue 2 is reconciled to {j.issue2.units}
            but remains a working layer: no 1927 scan of it is known to survive.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="act">
      <h2><span class="act-no">Method</span>Evidence and its limits</h2>
      <div class="method">
        <p>
          <b>Four witnesses.</b> A 1927 scan of Issue 1; a 2009 scholarly reprint that is the only text witness
          for Issue 2; the Yonsei serial-article catalogue; and the published bibliography. None is treated as
          ground truth, and every item records which of them attests it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Two layers of transmission.</b> All machine-readable text comes from an optical reading of the
          2009 reprint, which itself modernised spacing and marked what its editors could not read. Claims that
          depend on the 1927 text’s spacing, lineation or exact word counts are therefore not made.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Attribution, not authorship.</b> A byline is evidence that a text was published under a name. Every
          identity carries a status — confirmed, probable, or unresolved — and a shared pen name is never
          silently treated as a shared person.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Coding.</b> Place mentions are coded per episode under an eight-function scheme, by one coder, with
          a stratified audit of a fifth of the evidence. Decisions to exclude are published alongside the
          evidence they exclude: {j.not_counted} such records are listed in full.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="caveat">
        <p class="cap-kicker">What this edition does not yet claim</p>
        <p>
          Spatial coding covers Issue 1 only; Issue 2 is not yet coded, so nothing here describes the magazine
          as a whole. Explanations for the patterns in the coded evidence remain under test and are marked
          exploratory throughout — none is presented as established.
        </p>
        <p>
          Page images and full transcription are rights-gated: the 2009 edition is in copyright, and the terms
          of the 1927 scan are not yet established. What is published here is this edition’s own scholarship —
          structure, metadata, coded evidence, and short scholarly quotation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p style="font:var(--t-caption)/1.6 var(--sans);color:var(--muted);margin-top:var(--s-5)">
        Source: 손종흠·박경우·유춘동 편, <i>근대 기생의 문화와 예술: 자료편</i> (보고사, 2009), reproducing the
        Yonsei University Library copies; and a 2005 digitisation of the 1927 original of Issue 1.
        Coded data are offered under CC-BY-4.0; source texts remain with their rights holders.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Project>
